From 4d17eff997a0ddf9ab323eeb984de390a3fbf0e7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ANiri anland audio fix <anland-audio@localhost>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 00:28:07 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] backend/anland: take the audio socket out of the realtime
 callback

Follow-up to 89e6ceb. Sending from the RT process callback was never actually
safe, and several smaller defects came with it.

audio_fd was documented as loop-thread-only while on_capture_process() called
sendmsg(a->audio_fd, ...) on the data thread, and the loop thread concurrently
reassigned it, destroyed the io source (which closes it) and closed it. Making
it _Atomic int would have removed the C data race but not the lifetime problem:
the RT thread can load the descriptor, the loop thread can close it, the number
can be reused by an unrelated open(), and the RT thread then sends on somebody
else's fd.

So the socket is no longer touched on the data thread at all. A dedicated
sender thread owns it for its whole lifetime:

  * on_capture_process() builds one whole AUDIO_MSG_PCM message (real PCM, or
    the keep-alive silence block) and pushes it into a single-producer /
    single-consumer slot queue (head/tail are atomics with acquire/release
    pairing, one whole message per slot, never split, bounded at 64 KiB), then
    pokes a non-blocking pipe;
  * sender_thread() is the only code that calls sendmsg(). It drains the queue,
    waits for POLLOUT when the kernel buffer is full, and on EAGAIN keeps the
    slot instead of dropping it -- the ring's own bound is what applies
    back-pressure;
  * detach sets the keep-alive to zero, bumps the epoch, sets the stop flag,
    pokes the pipe and pthread_join()s the sender. Only then is anything closed.
    The sender's only blocking calls are poll() with a 100 ms timeout, so the
    join is bounded. Its last act is close(fd), so the loop thread never closes
    a descriptor another thread might still be using.

That also removes the sendmsg(-1, ...) hole: the RT callback no longer has any
descriptor to check, and the silence path is gated on keepalive_len instead of
on a descriptor value. A sender thread that cannot be created drops the
attachment rather than pretending playback works.

keepalive_len is now gated exactly as documented. anland_audio_start() and the
unconditional rebuild call are gone from the arming path: the length is only
published once the consumer has announced AUDIO_ROLE_PLAYBACK on this
attachment (arm_keepalive(), gated on play_format_known), it is zeroed on
detach, and build_pw() re-arms only when a transport is still attached AND the
playback format was already confirmed. A consumer whose device matches our
defaults still arms, because arming no longer sits behind the "format changed"
early return.

rt_pcm_seen / rt_keepalive_on are RT-private with no cross-thread writer. The
loop thread signals a new attachment by bumping an atomic epoch and the RT
callback resets them itself when the epoch changes, so a detach/reconnect
between two process cycles is still observed.

The tick timer is genuinely periodic now: pw_loop_update_timer() is given a
non-NULL interval and armed once, instead of a one-shot that returned without
re-arming when connected (which meant the RT event drain usually ran exactly
once).

The old defer_detach() publication order is gone with it; the remaining
publications are keepalive_len (release) before epoch (release), and every
detach orders the keep-alive reset before the sender teardown.

Unchanged by request: the mic stream still does NOT use
PW_STREAM_FLAG_RT_PROCESS, the mic ring remains loop-thread-only, the
SOCK_SEQPACKET framing checks are untouched, and the speaker keep-alive
behaviour is preserved. No other Niri code is touched.
